---
import Base from "../../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import sources from "../../../../data/soa-agentic-se/agentic-memory/sources.json";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const here = `${base}research/soa-agentic-se/agentic-memory/`;
type Rec = (typeof sources)[number];
const count = (f: (r: Rec) => boolean) => sources.filter(f).length;
const total = sources.length;
const bySector = { academic: count((r) => r.sector === "academic"), industry: count((r) => r.sector === "industry"), openSource: count((r) => r.sector === "open-source") };
const years = [2026, 2025, 2024, 2023];
const byYear = years.map((y) => [y, count((r) => r.year === y)] as const);
const consensusSurfaced = count((r) => (r.found_via ?? "").includes("consensus"));
const verified = count((r) => r.verification?.verdict === "CONFIRMED");
const minorFix = count((r) => r.verification?.verdict === "MINOR-FIX");
const problems = count((r) => ["NUMBER-NOT-FOUND", "NOT-FOUND"].includes(r.verification?.verdict ?? ""));
const hitlAny = count((r) => !!r.hitl && r.hitl !== "none");
const preWrite = count((r) => r.hitl === "pre-write-approval");
const humanAuthored = count((r) => (r.memory_coding?.write_policy ?? "") === "human-authored");
const validated = count((r) => !!r.memory_coding?.validation && r.memory_coding.validation !== "none");
const benchmarks = count((r) => r.category.includes("memory-benchmark"));
const security = count((r) => r.category.includes("memory-safety-privacy"));
---

<Base
  title="Jason Cusati — Agent Memory"
  description="State of the art on persistent memory for coding agents: who writes it, when, how it is validated and forgotten, what has been measured, and how humans stay in the loop — with a 283-record source explorer."
>
  <p class="crumbs"><a href={`${base}research/soa-agentic-se/`}>← SoA Agentic SE</a></p>
  <h1>Memory for Coding Agents</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    What persists when a coding agent's session ends — repository facts,
    preferences, episodes, skills, rules — and who gets to write it? A
    state-of-the-art digest across the academic literature and primary
    industry sources, with particular attention to human-in-the-loop
    governance, as of <strong>14 September 2026</strong>.
  </p>

  <div class="cards">
    <a class="card" href={`${here}synthesis/`}><span class="card-title">Read the synthesis</span><span class="card-desc">Vocabulary; where memory lives and who writes it; what has been measured (controlled results, benchmarks, failure modes, security); humans in the loop; gaps.</span></a>
    <a class="card" href={`${here}sources/`}><span class="card-title">Explore the {total} sources</span><span class="card-desc">Every record is coded on scope, storage, write policy, validation, evidence and human-in-the-loop mode — all filterable — plus discovery channel and verification verdict.</span></a>
    <a class="card" href={`${here}consensus/`}><span class="card-title">Consensus searches</span><span class="card-desc">Sixty queries run through the Consensus connector, logged with the papers each surfaced.</span></a>
  </div>

  <h2>The one-paragraph answer</h2>
  <p>
    The field has converged on a vocabulary — memory types × memory
    operations, with memory, skills and rules understood as one artifact at
    different compression ratios — but not on evidence. The best-controlled
    studies find that persistent memory does not raise coding-agent success
    rates by default: repository context files do not generally improve task
    success while adding over 20% inference cost, random rules match
    expert-curated ones, 39 of 49 real software-engineering skills give zero
    gain, and a pre-registered five-way memory comparison finds gains only
    when the new problem is a near-copy of a stored one. What does move the
    number is validation of the write — a tested-before-accept gate turns "no
    better than no skills" into a 48-point gain — and delivery of the read:
    agents left to choose make zero voluntary memory operations in 114 turns.
    Industry has reached the same conclusion from the other side: the one
    vendor with an A/B on a real engineering outcome gates memories with
    citation checks and an expiry; two vendors retrofitted per-memory human
    approval after shipping silent auto-memory; one retired auto-memory for
    human-authored skills. Nobody has yet run the experiment that matters for
    a governance stance: human-ratified versus execution-validated memory
    promotion, head to head, on the same repository tasks.
  </p>

  <h2>Dataset at a glance</h2>
  <table class="stats"><tbody>
    <tr><th scope="row">Records</th><td>{total} — {bySector.academic} academic, {bySector.industry} industry, {bySector.openSource} open-source</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">By year</th><td>{byYear.map(([y, n], i) => <span>{y}: {n}{i < byYear.length - 1 ? " · " : ""}</span>)}</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Human in the loop somewhere</th><td>{hitlAny} records; {preWrite} with pre-write approval; {humanAuthored} human-authored memory</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Write validated at all</th><td>{validated} records</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Benchmarks / security &amp; privacy</th><td>{benchmarks} / {security} records</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Surfaced by Consensus</th><td>{consensusSurfaced} records</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Independently re-verified</th><td>{verified} confirmed, {minorFix} minor metadata fixes, {problems} with a number or page not found, 0 wrong</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>

  <h2>Method</h2>
  <p>
    Four channels on the same day: a literature pass on memory mechanisms and
    taxonomies; a pass on benchmarks, failure modes and security; primary
    industry sources for every major coding agent and memory platform; and a
    human-in-the-loop and governance pass. The Consensus connector was the
    primary academic discovery channel, with arXiv abstract pages fetched for
    every number. A separate adversarially prompted verification agent
    re-fetched 112 of the records — every low- or medium-confidence record,
    every number quoted in the research memos, plus a random thirty — and its
    corrections were applied. Every record is coded on the paper's memory
    scheme (scope, storage, write policy, validation, evidence) plus a
    human-in-the-loop field, so the explorer can be filtered on exactly the
    questions the synthesis asks.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .crumbs { font-size: 0.9rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .lede { font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--color-muted); margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin: 1.5rem 0; }
  .card { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.4rem; padding: 1rem 1.1rem; border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-left: 4px solid var(--color-accent); border-radius: 4px; background: var(--color-surface); color: var(--color-text); text-decoration: none; }
  .card:hover { border-left-color: var(--color-primary); text-decoration: none; }
  .card-title { font-family: var(--font-sans); font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-primary); }
  .card-desc { font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--color-muted); }
  .stats { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: 0.95rem; }
  .stats th, .stats td { text-align: left; padding: 0.5rem 0.6rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); vertical-align: top; }
  .stats th { width: 40%; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-muted); }
</style>
